\f2\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec3 3. Four Folds\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec3 The recurring folds in earlier NEXAH work may be read as stages of description.\
Not as fixed mathematics, but as an architectural sequence:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs26 \cf0 \strokec3 \
FOLD I    distinction\
FOLD II   relation\
FOLD III  representation\
FOLD IV   return / reflection\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \strokec3 A sheet is not destroyed when folded.\
Its local surfaces change their orientation to one another.\
Something can disappear behind another layer while remaining present in the\uc0\u8232 whole sheet.\
This is central to perception.\
What disappears has not necessarily ceased to exist.\
It may merely be:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 occluded;\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 outside the selected scale;\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 outside the selected spectrum;\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 behind the observer;\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 excluded by the current projection;\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 compressed into a larger category.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec3 The fold creates visibility and invisibility simultaneously.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec4 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa321\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec3 4. The Forest\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec3 From far away, the forest may appear as one green surface.\
Or, humorously:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec3 mold on the landscape.\strokec3 \
At that scale, the grouping is useful.\
The observer approaches.\
The apparently uniform surface separates into:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs26 \cf0 \strokec3 \
forest\
\uc0\u8595 \
tree populations\
\uc0\u8595 \
individual trees\
\uc0\u8595 \
branches and leaves\
\uc0\u8595 \
animals and insects\
\uc0\u8595 \
fungi and mycelial networks\
\uc0\u8595 \
cells\
\uc0\u8595 \
molecules\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \strokec3 No single scale reveals 
\f3\i \strokec3 the complete forest
\f1\i0 \strokec3 .\
At each distance, another object becomes relevant.\
The forest is simultaneously:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 landscape;\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 habitat;\
