\f2\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec3 7. The Insect Field\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec3 Insects make this distinction especially visible.\
Their bodies do not merely transport a central observer.\
Their bodies participate in sensing.\
Compound eyes, antennae, sensory hairs, wings, legs, exoskeletons and segmented\uc0\u8232 bodies create a geometry of contact with the environment.\
Examples include:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 movement-sensitive compound vision;\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 ultraviolet sensitivity;\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 polarization sensitivity;\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 chemical trails;\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 air-flow sensing;\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 vibration through webs or surfaces;\
\ls7\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec3 rapid temporal resolution.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec3 The insect does not receive the same world as a mammal in a smaller body.\
Its available world is structured differently.\
This does not mean that each organism creates physical reality.\
It means that each organism accesses a different observational field.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

