\f2\b\fs48 \cf0 \strokec3 18. Operators as Seams\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 \strokec3 The simplest mathematical signs can be read architecturally.\
Not by replacing their established mathematical meanings, but by observing\uc0\u8232 their relational roles.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs26 \cf0 \strokec3 \
+   connect / aggregate\
\uc0\u8722    remove / separate / orient by difference\
=   identify under a declared criterion\
\uc0\u8800    preserve distinction\
0   neutral reference / cancellation result\
\uc0\u8594    map / transition / implication\
\uc0\u8596    reciprocal translation\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 \strokec3 In mathematics, each symbol receives meaning from its formal context.\
In the Codex, the recurring interest lies in what these signs 
\f3\i \strokec3 do between\uc0\u8232 terms
\f1\i0 \strokec3 .\
The focus shifts from operands to seams.\
Not:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec3 What are the two objects?\strokec3 \
but:\
\strokec3 What relation allows them to appear in one expression?\strokec3 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
